last night at the Calgary underground  Film Festival I saw under the shadow the  movie is Iranian though it wasn't made  in Iran it's in Farsi and it takes place  in Tehran in 1988 as the iran-iraq war  reached its peak and is a film that I'm  having a bit of trouble trying to  describe not because I don't know what  it's about but more because I can't  decide how I want to describe it what  layer of the film I want to present  because like all good horror films its  horror isn't just a creature but is the  ideas that that creature represents on  the surface level it is a supernatural  horror with a monster who terrorizes the  protagonists who tried desperately to  figure out how to fight back a bit of a  bottle movie meets ghost story on a  deeper level there is a private horror  about the personal and cultural  anxieties that the monster represents  however just saying it that way feels  like a disservice to the film I mean  saying that horror films are built on  metaphor is a pretty pedestrian  observation saying that monsters and  movies represent things that aren't  literally monsters is like okay  congratulations you understand horror as  a genre that's what a horror movie is so  what I want to stress is the level to  which understanding the metaphor of the  monster is so very key to understanding  the threat of the monster in this film  the xenomorph an alien is dangerous and  scary because it's made of teeth and  death and darkness and silence it kills  people  the sexual dimension of the film the  themes of penetration and violation are  largely subtextual they're not something  that the characters engage with much  outside the context of the xenomorph in  contrast to the monster and under the  shadow is the patriarchy and even though  it's not put in that exact word the  shadow of patriarchy looms large over  the lives of the characters it is a  movie about being small in a large world  about living in the shadow of things  that are so big so out of scope with a  single individual that they become  almost supernatural the main character  shidae lives in the shadow of the  Iranian Cultural Revolution the shadow  of the Ayatollah the shadow of the Iran  an Iraq war the shadow of  Americanization and on a more intimate  level the shadow of depression and  anxiety about her place in a large and  complex world her relationship with her  mother and her daughter an idealist and  a revolutionary in her youth shidae now  finds herself in a world where her  rights and freedoms are ever diminishing  where old superstitions have regained  their strength and are exerting their  power the fact that this is a ghost  story of sorts is important it is  poignant that the threat is mythology -  the thing that threatens today is a  cultural artifact the movie rides  feelings of anxiety depression  frustration and inadequacy and it rides  them hard large segments of the film are  basically the encapsulation of a panic  attack in film form the filmmakers make  heavy use of displace sounds to put the  audience off-kilter and it works it  works really well  the final notes of the film are  complicated I've spent quite a bit of  time digesting it at this point and the  ending is a bluff it's a very sad ending  wearing the skin of a kind of happy  ending this is intentional there is a  release of tension an escape from the  immediate threats but can you ever truly  escape yourself your past your family  can you escape the very culture that you  exist in can you escape myth itself
 

